== Normalized concept dossiers ==

=== Logic ===
Logic studies consequence, validity, inference, and formal systems for representing and evaluating reasoning. A logic commonly includes a language together with proof rules and/or semantics.

Classical logic is highly influential but is not the definition of logic as such. Intuitionistic, modal, relevant, many-valued, paraconsistent, temporal, epistemic, and other logics revise language, consequence, semantics, or proof rules.

'''Do not conflate:''' logic ≠ one universal notation; syntax ≠ semantics; derivability ≠ semantic consequence unless soundness/completeness conditions establish the connection.

'''Sources:''' Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy on Classical Logic; Open Logic Project; standard mathematical logic texts.

=== Deductive reasoning ===
Deductive reasoning aims at conclusions that follow necessarily from premises under a specified consequence relation.

A deductively valid argument cannot have true premises and a false conclusion under the relevant semantics.

'''Do not conflate:''' validity ≠ truth of premises; valid ≠ sound; deduction ≠ certainty in the psychological sense.

'''Sources:''' logic textbooks; SEP logical consequence / classical logic.

=== Inductive reasoning ===
Inductive reasoning is ampliative: its conclusion can go beyond what is logically entailed by its premises.

Forms include enumerative induction, statistical generalization, predictive inference, analogical induction, and probabilistic support.

Inductive support is defeasible and can be represented qualitatively or quantitatively depending on the framework.

'''Do not conflate:''' induction ≠ simple enumeration; induction ≠ deduction with weaker confidence; induction ≠ abduction.

'''Sources:''' SEP Inductive Logic; SEP Problem of Induction; statistical inference literature.

=== Abductive reasoning ===
Abductive reasoning uses explanatory considerations to generate or support hypotheses. In modern philosophy it is often associated with inference to the best explanation.

Abduction is generally ampliative and its normative status remains debated.

'''Do not conflate:''' best available explanation ≠ true explanation; abduction ≠ Bayesian inference, although connections can be modeled.

'''Sources:''' SEP Abduction, substantively revised 2025.

=== Argument ===
An argument is a structured set of claims in which premises are offered as reasons for a conclusion.

Arguments can be deductive, inductive, abductive, analogical, probabilistic, causal, or practical.

'''Do not conflate:''' argument ≠ dispute; premise/conclusion role ≠ truth value; validity applies most directly to deductive consequence, while inductive arguments use strength/support standards.

=== Proof ===
A mathematical proof is a finite or otherwise formally licensed demonstration that a conclusion follows from axioms, definitions, previously established results, and accepted inference rules.

Informal mathematical proof can be rigorous without being fully formalized symbol-by-symbol.

'''Do not conflate:''' proof ≠ empirical evidence; mathematical proof ≠ scientific confirmation; formal derivation ≠ every ordinary use of “proof”.

'''Sources:''' proof theory / mathematical logic references; Open Logic Project; Encyclopedia of Mathematics.

=== Conjecture ===
A conjecture is a mathematical statement proposed as true but not yet proved or disproved within the relevant mathematical framework.

Evidence, computation, analogy, and special cases can motivate a conjecture but do not constitute proof.

'''Do not conflate:''' conjecture ≠ theorem; conjecture ≠ scientific hypothesis by definition.

=== Contradiction ===
A contradiction in classical logic is commonly represented by a formula together with its negation, or a proposition of the form φ ∧ ¬φ.

In classical logic, contradictions interact with explosion: from inconsistency, arbitrary conclusions can be derived. Paraconsistent logics reject unrestricted explosion.

'''Do not conflate:''' contradiction ≠ disagreement; contradictory statements ≠ merely contrary statements.

=== Consistency (logic) ===
Consistency is a property of a set of statements or formal system indicating, under a given logic, that contradiction or triviality is not derivable.

In classical first-order settings, syntactic consistency and semantic satisfiability are linked by completeness.

'''Do not conflate:''' consistency ≠ truth; a consistent theory can be false or incomplete; relative consistency ≠ absolute proof from nowhere.

'''Sources:''' Encyclopedia of Mathematics; Open Logic Project; Gödel completeness/incompleteness literature.

=== Probability ===
Probability is a mathematical framework for representing chance, uncertainty, or degrees of support under specified interpretations and axioms.

Kolmogorov-style axioms provide a standard mathematical calculus, but interpretations of probability include frequentist, propensity, subjective/Bayesian, logical, and other views.

'''Do not conflate:''' probability ≠ frequency by definition; mathematical probability calculus ≠ one philosophical interpretation.

=== Probability distribution ===
A probability distribution assigns probabilities to possible values or events of a random variable.

Discrete distributions use probability mass functions; continuous distributions are often described with density functions whose integrals yield probabilities.

'''Do not conflate:''' density value ≠ probability at a point; distribution ≠ observed histogram; fitted distribution ≠ guaranteed data-generating truth.

'''Sources:''' NIST/SEMATECH Engineering Statistics Handbook; probability texts.

=== Correlation ===
Correlation describes statistical association between variables. Pearson correlation measures linear association; other measures capture rank, nonlinear, categorical, or dependence structures.

'''Do not conflate:''' correlation ≠ causation; Pearson r = 0 ≠ independence in general; high correlation ≠ stable relationship under intervention or distribution shift.

'''Sources:''' NIST statistics material; standard statistical inference texts.

=== Conditional probability ===
Conditional probability P(A|B) is the probability of A given that B is taken as given, with the standard finite-probability definition P(A∩B)/P(B) when P(B)>0.

Conditioning changes the probability space under consideration but does not automatically represent a causal intervention.

'''Do not conflate:''' P(A|B) ≠ P(B|A); conditional association ≠ causal effect.

=== Bayesian inference ===
Bayesian inference combines a prior distribution with a likelihood model to obtain a posterior distribution through Bayes' rule.

Posterior inference can support prediction, parameter estimation, model comparison, and decision making.

'''Do not conflate:''' Bayes' theorem ≠ Bayesian inference as a full modeling workflow; likelihood ≠ probability of a hypothesis; prior ≠ necessarily subjective or arbitrary.

'''Sources:''' SEP Bayesian Epistemology; SEP Inductive Logic; statistical Bayesian texts.

=== Belief revision ===
Belief revision is the formal study of how a body of full beliefs should change when new information is added or removed.

The AGM tradition distinguishes expansion, contraction, and revision and represents rationality constraints through postulates and representation theorems.

Contemporary belief-revision research also includes belief bases, iterated revision, non-prioritized revision, and probabilistic relations.

'''Do not conflate:''' belief revision ≠ Bayesian conditionalization; full belief sets ≠ graded credences; formal revision ≠ human memory editing.

'''Sources:''' SEP Logic of Belief Revision, substantively revised 2026; Alchourrón, Gärdenfors & Makinson (1985).

=== Propositional logic ===
Propositional logic studies formulas built from proposition/sentence variables and truth-functional or related propositional connectives.

Classical propositional logic commonly uses negation, conjunction, disjunction, conditional, and biconditional.

'''Do not conflate:''' propositional logic ≠ all logic; propositional variables do not represent internal predicate/quantifier structure.

'''Sources:''' SEP Propositional Logic; Open Logic Project.

=== First-order logic ===
First-order logic extends propositional structure with predicates, terms, variables, identity (in common formulations), and quantification over individuals.

It occupies a central place in mathematical logic because of its expressive power and metalogical properties including soundness, completeness, compactness, and Löwenheim–Skolem results.

'''Do not conflate:''' first-order quantification ≠ quantification over predicates/properties; first-order logic ≠ second-order logic.

'''Title note:''' “predicate logic” and “predicate calculus” are historical/common alternatives but are not used as the canonical page title because they can be broader or ambiguous.

'''Sources:''' SEP Classical Logic; SEP Emergence of First-Order Logic; Open Logic Project.

=== Modal logic ===
Modal logic extends logical languages with operators interpreted using notions such as necessity and possibility. The same formal architecture supports temporal, epistemic, deontic, doxastic, dynamic, and other modalities.

Kripke semantics interprets modal formulas over possible worlds/states connected by an accessibility relation.

'''Do not conflate:''' modal logic ≠ one axiom system; necessity ≠ high probability; epistemic modality ≠ alethic modality.

'''Sources:''' modal logic texts; SEP modal/epistemic logic; Open Logic Project.

