== Source-family audit ==

* '''Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy''' — classical/propositional logic, induction, abduction, Bayesian epistemology, belief revision, modal/epistemic logic.
* '''Open Logic Project''' — rigorous formal syntax/semantics/proof systems for propositional and first-order logic, consistency, soundness/completeness.
* '''Encyclopedia of Mathematics''' — proof theory, consistency, mathematical logic.
* '''NIST/SEMATECH Engineering Statistics Handbook''' — distributions, statistical modeling, correlation-oriented reference material.
* '''Standard probability/statistics texts''' — probability axioms, random variables, distributions, conditional probability, Bayesian inference.
* '''Primary foundational sources''' — Bayes/Laplace; Kolmogorov; Peirce; Hilbert; Gödel; Tarski; Kripke; AGM — only when tied to specific historical/theoretical claims.
* Wikipedia is discovery/orientation only where stronger sources exist.

== Title and identity risks ==

* [[Logic]] must not imply classical logic is the only logic.
* [[Reasoning]] already exists in Wave 4; Wave 5 descendants must link back rather than redefine the psychological umbrella.
* [[Argument]] must not be written as rhetorical conflict.
* [[Proof]] must keep formal mathematical proof distinct from empirical “proof”.
* [[Conjecture]] must remain mathematical, not silently become a synonym for [[Hypothesis]].
* [[Consistency (logic)]] prevents collision with future database/distributed-system consistency.
* [[Correlation]] must not be reduced to Pearson correlation.
* [[Bayesian inference]] must distinguish Bayes' theorem from the full inferential framework.
* [[Belief revision]] must not be reduced to AGM only; AGM is the central framework, not the entire field.
* [[First-order logic]] replaces provisional [[Predicate logic]] as canonical identity.
* [[Modal logic]] must not treat possible-world semantics as the only conceivable semantics.

